# UI Testing Standards

Wave D / Wave E established a three-layer test pyramid for UI work.
Every UI Design Input MUST pin the right layer in its
`verification.test_protocol`.

```
1. jest static-source VT      -- chrome, imports, structural invariants (fast, deterministic)
2. jest live-render VT        -- isolated primitive behaviour (limited blast radius)
3. Playwright + axe e2e       -- user journeys + WCAG 2.1 AA (slow, full-stack)
```

---

## Layer 1 -- Jest static-source VTs (the workhorse)

Most pages we ship hit live FastAPI / Neo4j / NICE endpoints on mount,
which makes a unit-scoped live render fragile. The fix is to read the
component source as a string and assert structural invariants with
ripgrep-style regexes. This is the canonical Wave E pattern.

### When to use

- Verifying a page imports the right `@leo/design-system` primitives.
- Verifying legacy chrome (Card / CardContent / SectionBox / raw
  TextField) is removed.
- Verifying a card uses canonical tokens (12px radius, 18px padding,
  3px accent strip, 168px minHeight, framer-motion stagger).
- Verifying handlers / API calls / router pushes are preserved
  through a refactor (`api.get('/fhir/PlanDefinition')`,
  `router.push('/admin/pathways/...')`).
- Verifying a page exposes the canonical loading / empty / no-match
  copy strings.

### Canonical skeleton

```tsx
/**
 * VT-LEO-AI-XXX -- <one-line summary of what is being verified>.
 *
 * Static-source verification:
 *   - <bullet 1>
 *   - <bullet 2>
 *
 * Design Controls: DI-LEO-AI-XXX, DO-LEO-AI-XXX, VT-LEO-AI-XXX.
 */

import * as fs from "fs";
import * as path from "path";

const REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(__dirname, "../../../..");

function readFile(relPath: string): string {
  return fs.readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, relPath), "utf8");
}

describe("VT-LEO-AI-XXX - <feature> chrome", () => {
  test("TC-001: imports the right design-system primitives", () => {
    const src = readFile("services/ui/src/app/admin/<feature>/page.tsx");
    expect(src).toMatch(/from\s+["']@leo\/design-system["']/);
    expect(src).toMatch(/StatHero/);
    expect(src).toMatch(/SegmentedTabs/);
    expect(src).toMatch(/ToolbarActions/);
  });

  test("TC-002: legacy chrome removed", () => {
    const src = readFile("services/ui/src/app/admin/<feature>/page.tsx");
    expect(src).not.toMatch(/<Typography variant="h5">/);
    expect(src).not.toMatch(/import\s+Card\s+from\s+"@mui\/material\/Card"/);
  });

  // ... more cases
});
```

### Regex discipline (lessons learned)

Static-source regexes break easily when prettier wraps long lines.
Always:

- Use `[\s\S]*?` (NOT `.*?`) when matching across newlines.
- Insert `\\s*` between adjacent tokens (e.g. between `>` of a
  generic and `(` of a call): `\.get<[\s\S]*?>\s*\(\s*"\/fhir\/`.
- Anchor on stable strings (route paths, API endpoints, design-token
  values) -- never on whitespace-sensitive layout.
- Extract a JSX tag with `src.match(/<MyComponent[\s\S]*?\/>/)` then
  assert on its props -- much more robust than one giant regex.

### Test case naming

- Suite name: `VT-LEO-AI-<NNN> - <short title>`.
- Test name: `TC-<NNN>: <observable behaviour>`.
- The test names MUST match the `case_name` field in the VT YAML
  one-for-one.

### Recording execution evidence in the VT YAML

After running, append to `execution_history`:

```yaml
execution_history:
  - executed_at: "<YYYY-MM-DD>"
    executed_by: "Wave E Phase <N> automation"
    result: "pass"
    notes: "All <N> test cases pass under jest 30; 0 failures."
```

Set `status: "passed"` only when the run is green.

